gpui.f.welcome = Welcome to Backplane
gpui.f.welcome.body = Backplane runs coding agents on your boards, schematics and mechanical parts, and draws the results in its own viewers. This setup checks what you have, helps you add what is missing and ends with a tour of each feature. Every step can be skipped. Enter moves on. Esc is Finish later, which brings you back to this step at the next launch. End setup stops it for good. Press a step in the list to go straight to it. Settings, Setup runs all of this again.
gpui.f.installed = Installed
gpui.f.install.body.linux = You are running Backplane {version} on Linux. The installer puts Backplane in ~/.local/share/backplane, links the backplane command into ~/.local/bin and adds Backplane to your applications menu. Closing the window leaves the hub running, so agents finish their work.
gpui.f.install.body.darwin = You are running Backplane {version} on macOS. The app is in Applications, or in the folder where you unpacked it. macOS builds are not notarized yet, so macOS asks you to approve the app once. If it refused to open, use Open Anyway in System Settings, Privacy and Security. Closing the window leaves the hub running, so agents finish their work.
gpui.f.install.body.windows = You are running Backplane {version} on Windows. Backplane is installed in your user folder, under AppData, Local, Backplane. Its guard keeps it updated and brings the hub back if it stops. The Start menu opens it. Closing the window leaves the hub running, so agents finish their work. Windows may ask for permission once when a tool's own installer starts.
